  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1212 AM CDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nShowers, perhaps a rumble of thunder overnight\n\n2 disturbances, the first over IA and the second over western MN, \nwill roll eastward overnight. Moistening atmosphere ahead of these \nwaves, as evidenced by spreading fog and stratus, should allow for \nsome showers to develop with these potentially (45-90%) occurring in \nour CWA. Progged soundings show just a touch of instability ahead of \nthese waves so a few rumbles of thunder (5-15% chance at a given \nlocation) could occur, an outcome borne out by a bit of sporadic \nlightning ongoing in central MN as of 03z. With this instability \nbeing modest and elevated, am not concerned about any strong to \nsevere storms. Additionally, overall forcing is weak and \nprogressive, so flooding concerns are minimal as well.\n\nWidespread rain Friday into Saturday\n\nUpper low barrels eastward from OR eastward to WY Friday into \nSaturday, leading to lee pressure falls in the central High Plains. \nAs this occurs, surface high builds in ON/QC in the wake of an upper \nlow moving from James Bay to Atlantic Canada. Continued easterly \nflow across much of the Great Lakes coupled with southwesterly flow \nacross the southern and central Plains looks to reinforce a front \nwith this feature becoming quasi-stationary somewhere in the \nsouthern Upper Midwest. Stout upper high over the low Mississippi \nValley will also help drive that southwesterly low level flow which \nwill advect large amounts of moisture from the Gulf to the region - \nPWATs are progged to reach the high end of GEFS/ECENS climatology on \nthe 16.12z runs. This moist 850/700mb flow looks to be roughly \nnormal to the front and, with upper support in the form of a lead \ndisturbance Friday and an additional shortwave Saturday, locations \nnear the stalled front will see multiple (25th-75th values in 17.01z \nNBM are around 2-4\") inches of rain with potential (15%) for more \nthan 5\". Localized flooding would be possible with this amount of \nrain. Main question remaining appears to be exactly where that quasi-\nstationary front sets up. Unfortunately, vast majority of guidance \nhas it somewhere in our forecast area. Once the location becomes a \nbit more clearer, a Flood Watch may be needed ahead of Friday.\n\nAdditional rain chances next week\n\nAfter the heavy rain threat departs to the east on Saturday, the \nforecast area will be in-between that system and another weather \nsystem approaching from the west. An H300 cutoff low pressure \nsystem will continue to slowly trek east late this weekend into \nnext week. Timing and evolution of the low pressure vary in the \nensemble guidance, which leads to uncertainty regarding the \ntiming and location of any potential rainfall on Sunday into \nMonday. Given the low confidence, will keep the broad PoPs from \nSunday into Monday. PWAT values will be a bit lower than the \nprevious few days so the potential for heavier rain will be \nlesser with the next system. Even with that being said, the \nsaturated soils and higher river levels will make it easier for \nflooding to occur so that may be an impact once again for the \nsecond system. After that system moves over the region, the \nforecast upper level pattern gets more uncertain as there is \nquite a bit of model disagreement at this time. Through this \ntime frame, temperatures should remain slightly below average to\nnear average for late this weekend into the middle of the next \nwork week.",
